Current Reality
This UI is NOT useful for a real maintenance person. Here's why:
What It Shows vs What They Need
| What We Have | What They Need |
|--------------|----------------|
| Historical events that already happened | Real-time alerts - "Turbine X is acting strange NOW" |
| CARE scores (model evaluation metrics) | Risk scores - "Turbine Y will likely fail in 2 weeks" |
| Past anomaly detection | Predictions - "Check turbine Z before it fails" |
The Fundamental Problem
Our model does Anomaly Detection (detecting that something is already wrong), not Failure Prediction (predicting when something will fail).
The CARE dataset is for benchmarking anomaly detection algorithms - it's not a real-time monitoring system.
---
What Would Make It Useful
For a maintenance person, you'd need:
1. Live sensor data stream - Not in our dataset
2. RUL (Remaining Useful Life) predictions - Different model, different data
3. Risk scoring per turbine - "Fix this first"
4. Maintenance scheduling - "Which turbines should we inspect this week?"
---
Honest Assessment
This is a research prototype demonstrating:
- How to build an anomaly detection benchmark
- How to train and evaluate models
- How to create a UI for viewing results
It's NOT a production maintenance system.
